WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.739 --> 00:00:32.009
Donald Glover’s music video for This is
America and tv show Atlanta, use similar methods

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.510
to make unique social commentary.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.649
The director of the music video Hiro Murai,
is also a producer and frequent director on

00:00:39.649 --> 00:00:45.630
the show, so let’s look at how this powerhouse
team uses Juxtaposition, Surrealism, Deadpan

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:51.100
Tone, and Symbolism in both the show and music
video to create some of the most artful social

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.039
commentary I’ve seen recently.

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:55.930
Juxtaposition
Both the show and the video, use the juxtaposition

00:00:55.930 --> 00:01:00.170
of entertainment or humor against difficult
and violent realities.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.540
In This is America the Juxtaposition is spacial
and temporal.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:09.459
The focus remains on the dancing, while serious
problems manifest themselves in the background,

00:01:09.459 --> 00:01:10.459
just out of our sight.

00:01:10.459 --> 00:01:27.049
But the main character also shifts suddenly
between being the perpetrator and the entertainment.

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:31.780
This scene uses the juxtaposition of humor,
commercialism, and violence.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:37.700
A joke that almost appears to be product placement,
is immediately followed by an image of violence,

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:51.159
and the whole scene is framed by the sound
of police sirens in the background.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:02:03.179
Surrealism:
One of the tools used to create juxtaposition

00:02:03.179 --> 00:02:05.630
in both works is Surrealism.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.649
Take a look at this scene, which highlights
the sudden risk everyone in a house is put

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:12.640
under when the police are called about a domestic
dispute.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.890
Even the two innocent bystanders in the house
are concerned about their safety when the

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:23.000
police show up.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:34.069
But this reality is constantly in contrast
with the absurdist humor of 

00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:43.590
the scene.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:48.709
By including surreal elements, the viewer
is forced to examine every part of the scene

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:57.650
from a fresh perspective, possibly causing
them to re-examine issues that had started

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.549
to seem normal.

00:02:59.549 --> 00:03:04.280
This forces the viewer to come face to face
with the social issues that the creators want

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:05.600
to highlight.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.970
As the music video’s central theme points
out, America has a focus problem.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.709
Often ignoring the issues, or moving on from
them very quickly.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.989
Because of this, embedding a social commentary
inside an entertaining song or show makes

00:03:18.989 --> 00:03:25.170
it more likely to catch peoples attention.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:33.780
Had Glover merely made a music video containing
the contents of the background, would people

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.180
be talking about it as much?

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.400
Deadpan Tone:
The juxtaposition and surrealism are emphasized

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.310
further by the deadpan delivery.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:50.449
Within the music video and show, characters
often treat shocking or surreal events as

00:03:50.449 --> 00:03:52.810
normal, barely reacting.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:57.570
Sometimes characters on the show recognize
the absurdity of a situation but much is still

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.570
left unacknowledged.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:04.060
In the music video, that role is left entirely
to the audience, by not acknowledging the

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.970
surreal or shocking elements, the viewer isn’t
told what to think or feel about them.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:12.650
It provokes you to think about an issue, instead
of telling you what to think about it.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.739
This deadpan tone is present in the visual
styles of both the show and the music video,

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:21.880
while the show is shot digitally and the music
video was show on film, Hiro Murai creates

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.360
a similar look for both.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.370
Using high grain and noise profiles and coloring
to create a look often associated with “gritty

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.370
realism”.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.391
Showing surrealist imagery through a look
associated with realism reinforces the juxtaposition

00:04:35.391 --> 00:04:36.950
central to both works.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:40.240
Symbolism:
The juxtaposition, surrealism and tone all

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.910
cause the viewer to look a little closer at
the works.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:46.300
And when they do, they’ll find more there
to uncover.

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:50.349
Both use symbolism to guide the viewer in
thinking deeper and harder about the subject

00:04:50.349 --> 00:04:51.349
matter.

00:04:51.349 --> 00:04:55.710
Some of it is fairly accessible and apparent,
like the Jim Crow poses in the video, or the

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:00.349
fact that Atlanta’s protagonist is named
Earn, and the primary conflict for the character

00:05:00.349 --> 00:05:05.560
revolves around earning a living.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.190
Use of symbolism asks for and necessitates
a certain amount of interpretation.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:15.660
The use of juxtaposition, surrealism, and
symbolism help form the social commentary,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.210
but they make it difficult for viewers to
find a single clear message as part of the

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:24.190
works, instead opening them up for multiple
interpretations.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.330
This isn’t a criticism, inability to find
a single straightforward message can cause

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:36.310
audience discomfort, but that’s reflective
of the discomforting complexities present

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.129
in the places these works are creating a portrait
of.

00:05:40.129 --> 00:05:46.249
The video and show exist together as a body
of works that are illustrative of the places

00:05:46.249 --> 00:05:47.479
who’s name they bear.

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:50.909
Forcing us to reexamine elements of those
places that have become normal to us, and

00:05:50.909 --> 00:06:04.869
take a closer look at what surrounds us.

